    "notes": "North Haven-born electrical engineer whose work at General Electric and RCA helped make long-distance radio reception more directional and less vulnerable to interference. His 1921 U.S. patent describes the receiving system later known as the Beverage antenna: a long horizontal wire oriented toward the desired signal so that waves arriving along the wire reinforce one another while much unwanted interference is reduced. He graduated from the University of Maine in 1915 and received the IRE Medal of Honor in 1945.",
